{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqprmyw46tn","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"cmm onboard lifts subconscious KB recall 7x at lower token cost but embedding throughput is the bottleneck","summary":"Validated the cmm-as-onboard-engine idea on the 13-query code-nav corpus. Extracted 1321 functions from zonoid lib+routes via codebase-memory-mcp (instant) and injected them as notes (title=name, summary=signature+file) into an isolated scratch workspace KB (D:/zonoid-cmm-onboard) via POST /overlay/note, then measured the subconscious /search arm over it. Even with a PARTIAL onboard (~600-950 of 1321 notes landed before abort), subconscious mean recall jumped from 0.045 (un-onboarded, orchestrator self-knowledge only) to 0.327 at LOWER token cost (580 -> 312), hitting 7/13 queries vs 2/13. That already matches the naive grep+read arm's recall (0.321) at ~1/10 the tokens (312 vs 3026). The remaining 0-recall queries are ones whose functions were not yet injected (partial set), not retrieval failures, so a full onboard should approach/exceed the cmm structural arm's 0.506. KEY ARCHITECTURAL FINDING: the onboard bottleneck is NOT extraction (cmm is instant) but the KB's synchronous per-note embedding throughput — injecting ~1300 notes at concurrency 16 each triggering a MiniLM embed saturated the daemon into unresponsiveness (/health timed out >5s); killing the injection let it recover in ~28s. A first-party cmm-style onboard pipeline therefore needs a batched/throttled/async bulk-embed path, not note-at-a-time /overlay/note injection. Implication for the build-vs-wrap decision: absorb only cmm's AST extraction layer (the rest — graph store, embeddings, query API — is redundant with the orchestrator KB), and add a throttled bulk-ingest path.","category":"project","tags":["benchmark","codebase-memory-mcp","onboard","kb","result","throughput"],"created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-22T22:07:06.532Z","vec":[-0.04926082119345665,-0.027541669085621834,0.036859460175037384,0.0